# Spec and PR

## Spec structure (order)

1. **Objective** — problem and single most important outcome.
2. **Inputs** — entry points (UI, routes, store, services); required/optional; formats.
3. **Outputs** — UI changes, state updates, side effects, return shapes.
4. **Edge cases** — empty, error, boundary, invalid inputs.
5. **Architectural constraints** — layer rules, prohibited deps, placement of IO and logic.
6. **Acceptance criteria** — testable Given/When/Then; pass/fail; happy path and edges.

Functional (user-visible) first; technical (data flow, layers) after, in same doc if needed. Plain, testable language; no implementation details or undefined terms.

## Spec template (sections to include)

Context, Functional description, Input/Output contracts, Possible states, Expected errors, Architectural constraints, Involved layers, Testable acceptance criteria, Out of scope. Version specs in header; map every acceptance criterion to tests; update spec and tests together in the same PR when behavior changes.

## SDD

- Feature change starts with a spec; behavior defined before code.
- Tests trace to spec acceptance criteria. Architecture rules in specs are binding.

## PR

- Max **200 lines** of production code change per PR (docs/tests/generated don’t count). Max **500 lines** per file. Split larger work into multiple PRs.
- **Behavior changes** (features, user-visible, business rules, data flow): spec required; acceptance criteria map to tests; spec and test updates in same PR. Docs-only, refactor-only, maintenance-only: no spec required (state explicitly).
- No architecture violations; no ambiguous or hidden requirements; all new behavior covered by tests.
